# Design System — COARSE-ALIGN-X

## Color tokens (never deviate without asking first)
- `--void`: #070912           background, deep field
- `--field`: #141a30          panel/surface background
- `--lock-cyan`: #7fd4e8      primary accent, confirmed/locked state ONLY
- `--disturbance-amber`: #e8a15c   RESERVED exclusively for active disturbance/degraded state — never decorative
- `--confirm-green`: #6fe8a8       RESERVED exclusively for successful acquisition/lock confirmation — never decorative
- `--lost-red`: #e86f7f            RESERVED exclusively for lost-lock/failure state — never decorative

Rule: amber, green, and red are STATE colors, not palette colors. If you are about to use one
decoratively (a button, a header accent, a hover state with no semantic meaning), stop and use
lock-cyan or a neutral gray instead. A color that means something everywhere means nothing.

## Typography
- Display / headings: Space Grotesk or Bricolage Grotesque
- ALL numeric/telemetry values (metrics, counters, live data): JetBrains Mono or Space Mono —
  this is non-negotiable, every number in this project renders in monospace, no exceptions
- Body text: General Sans (Fontshare) or an equivalent humanist sans

BANNED, do not use under any circumstance: Inter, Roboto, Open Sans, Arial, or any default
system font as a primary typeface. These read as generic/AI-templated.

## Motion principles
- No bounce, no elastic/spring overshoot easing, anywhere. Use restrained cubic-bezier easing only.
- All movement is damped (exponential smoothing), never snapped/instant, except for hard state
  transitions (e.g. lock confirmed) where a single sharp cut IS the correct emphasis.
- One element animates at a time per moment of attention. Do not animate multiple unrelated
  UI elements simultaneously "for effect."

## Cursor / pointer
- Custom reticle-style cursor across interactive surfaces where feasible, tightening slightly on
  hover over interactive elements — thematically a "confidence tightening on lock," not decorative.

## Before generating any new UI element
Ask: does this color/font/motion choice already exist in this system? If you're about to introduce
a new color, a new typeface, or a bounce/spring easing not listed above, STOP and ask the user first
rather than defaulting to a generic choice.
